From e0aca120ac00f9b754b470f8380346b237ce6f49 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 10:41:06 +0000
Subject: Follow range requests back to the whole file, and offer the audio too

Two things were wrong with what the previous change offered on a site that
streams adaptively.

The first is that the URL of a fetch is not always the URL of a file. Most
players ask for byte ranges with the Range header, and then the URL already
names the whole thing, but some sites put the range in the query string
instead, and there the URL names a slice. Downloading it gets a few seconds of
video. The bytestart/byteend pair is stripped for exactly that reason: with
those two parameters removed the same URL answers 200 with the full
Content-Length, which was checked against a real response rather than assumed.
No other parameter is touched, and no other range convention is guessed at,
because a signed URL stops working the moment you remove the wrong thing.

Stripping the range also makes the accounting honest. Every range of a file now
collapses onto one entry, and their sizes add up instead of one overwriting
another, so the number attached to a candidate is how much of it the page
actually pulled. That is the right way to tell which of several renditions is
the one being watched.

The second thing is that adaptive streaming splits the sound off into its own
file, so the video is silent on its own. The registry now finds the audio track
that goes with the chosen video, matched by host, and the menu offers it as a
second item. Two files rather than one, which is honest about what the page has:
it is not a muxed download, and it is not pretending to be one.

Two kinds of URL are now refused outright. A playlist or manifest, .m3u8 or
.mpd, is a text file listing where the pieces are, and saving it saves nothing
anyone can play; a lone .m4s or .ts segment is no better. Assembling a segmented
stream into a single file is a job this does not do. Media behind DRM is out of
scope entirely: nothing here decrypts anything, and nothing here goes looking
for a way around an access control.

Images are no longer recorded at all. Upstream already offers a download for an
image the person is pointing at, so there was nothing to add, and on a feed the
sheer number of them could push the video out of a list capped at 64.
